INT. CASINO BOAT - COUNTING ROOM - NIGHT

A windowless steel room that rocks faintly with the river. Money cages, bins of chips, a long table piled with banded cash. A brass clock ticks too loudly.

SAUL VERDUGO, sixties, the pit boss, sits at the head of the table with his hands flat. Facing him are MARISOL TREJO, thirties, with a leather folio; BAXTER KWAN, forties, in a damp raincoat; and DESMOND "DEZ" MOURAD, fifty, whose jacket hangs open to show a holster. At the cage, a cashier, LUCILLE FONTAINE, forties, has frozen with a stack of twenties in her hand. In the corner by the door, a boy, GABRIEL VERDUGO, sixteen, holds a mop and says nothing.

SAUL
Count it again.

MARISOL
We counted it twice.

SAUL
Then a third time won't hurt.

BAXTER
It's short two hundred thousand. You can count until the boat sinks.

SAUL
The boat is not going to sink, Mr. Kwan.

DEZ
Somebody in this room is lying.

MARISOL
Somebody on this boat is lying.

DEZ
That's what I said.

SAUL
No, it isn't. You said this room. This room has six people in it, one of them a child.

GABRIEL
I'm sixteen.

SAUL
You're a child.

BAXTER
The boy doesn't count.

MARISOL
Everyone counts. That's the point.

DEZ
Then let's start with the cage.

LUCILLE
Don't look at me.

DEZ
I'm looking at everyone.

SAUL
Lucille, put down the money.

Lucille sets the stack on the counter. Dez rises. Baxter rises. Marisol closes her folio. Saul lifts one hand, and the others stop where they stand.

SAUL (CONT'D)
Sit down. All of you. Sit down and we will do this like family.

Gabriel drops the mop. The clatter makes every head turn. Dez's hand goes to the holster. Lucille ducks behind the cage. Baxter backs against the wall. Marisol does not flinch.

Saul looks at his grandson, and the old man's face goes slack.
